  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday through next Saturday)\nIssued at 1217 PM CDT Sun Oct 18 2026\n\nMedium-long range model data is currently in rather fair agreement \nregarding the synoptic evolution through mid week. Zonal flow will \nreamplify into southwest flow and week ridging over the region as a \ndeepening low pressure system approaches the western seaboard. In \ngeneral, weather across the Panhandles will be more or less \nuneventful. Mild temperatures in the 70s to low 80s will recur each \nafternoon, with warmest days expected Wed and Thu. A tighter surface \npressure gradient will keep winds typically breezy for Panhandle \nstandards, out of the south-southwest at 15-25 mph gusting around 35 \nmph daily.\n\nLater in the week, global guidance is focusing on the aforementioned \ndeepening low to eventually eject over the Plains late this week \ninto next weekend. Ample disagreement exists on the exact track and \ntiming of this system, but early indications support the return of \nbetter moisture and rain chances to the Panhandles. Latest trends \npoint to better lift being in place over New Mexico, while better \nmoisture will be focused atop the Panhandles. In general, this \nplaces relatively higher confidence in rain chances over the western \ncombined Panhandles. However, if we can hold onto moisture as the \nsystem lifts over the Panhandles through the weekend, deterministic \nand ensemble outputs suggest we could see more widespread coverage \nof rainfall. \n\nHarrel",
